1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,673
Every year, thousands of people drive to this mountain in northern California.

2
00:00:05,673 --> 00:00:06,905
Some come to hike.

3
00:00:06,905 --> 00:00:08,412
Some come to meditate.

4
00:00:08,412 --> 00:00:14,300
And some come because they believe a hidden city of ancient survivors lives inside it.

5
00:00:14,766 --> 00:00:18,372
They say its people are tall, wise, and robed in white.

6
00:00:18,372 --> 00:00:22,306
That they escaped a continent that sank beneath the Pacific.

7
00:00:22,306 --> 00:00:26,699
That they have been waiting under Shasta for twelve thousand years.

8
00:00:27,166 --> 00:00:28,782
It is a wonderful story.

9
00:00:28,782 --> 00:00:31,274
So tonight we will not argue with it.

10
00:00:31,274 --> 00:00:37,200
We will draw its map, one layer at a time, and ask who first put each line on the paper.

11
00:00:37,666 --> 00:00:40,717
Some layers will turn out to be real science.

12
00:00:40,717 --> 00:00:42,412
Some will be pure legend.

13
00:00:42,412 --> 00:00:47,633
And one will lead us to a teenage boy with a pencil in eighteen eighty-three.

14
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:52,830
This is the case of Mount Shasta and the lost land of Lemuria.

15
00:00:52,830 --> 00:00:54,433
Evidence on one side.

16
00:00:54,433 --> 00:00:55,959
Theory on the other.

17
00:00:55,959 --> 00:00:57,866
And a verdict at the end.

18
00:01:02,333 --> 00:01:03,110
Layer one.

19
00:01:03,110 --> 00:01:04,042
The terrain.

20
00:01:04,042 --> 00:01:07,229
Mount Shasta is real, and it is enormous.

21
00:01:07,229 --> 00:01:15,466
A volcano rising to about forty-three hundred metres, standing alone above the forests of Siskiyou County.

22
00:01:15,933 --> 00:01:20,090
It has two main cones, active glaciers, and hot springs.

23
00:01:20,090 --> 00:01:24,321
It has erupted many times in the last few thousand years.

24
00:01:24,321 --> 00:01:29,666
Geologists call it one of the most impressive volcanoes in the Cascades.

25
00:01:30,133 --> 00:01:33,219
Because it stands alone, it makes its own weather.

26
00:01:33,219 --> 00:01:39,700
Lens-shaped clouds often sit on the summit, and in the right light they look like something landed there.

27
00:01:40,166 --> 00:01:43,920
Long before any city legend, the mountain was sacred.

28
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:51,500
The peoples of the region, including the Shasta, Modoc and Klamath, held it as a place of spirit and power.

29
00:01:51,966 --> 00:01:55,244
One Klamath story tells of two spirit chiefs.

30
00:01:55,244 --> 00:02:00,925
Skell, who lived on Shasta, and Llao, who lived under what is now Crater Lake.

31
00:02:00,925 --> 00:02:04,566
Their war threw fire and hot rock across the land.

32
00:02:05,033 --> 00:02:13,496
Many scholars think that story remembers a real eruption, the collapse of Mount Mazama, nearly eight thousand years ago.

33
00:02:13,496 --> 00:02:15,541
A myth that carried a memory.

34
00:02:15,541 --> 00:02:18,433
That is evidence of something remarkable.

35
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:22,143
But notice what is missing from these stories.

36
00:02:22,143 --> 00:02:23,835
There is no hidden city.

37
00:02:23,835 --> 00:02:24,893
No white robes.

38
00:02:24,893 --> 00:02:26,303
No sunken continent.

39
00:02:26,303 --> 00:02:29,900
Those lines are not on the oldest layer of the map.

40
00:02:30,366 --> 00:02:31,114
Layer two.

41
00:02:31,114 --> 00:02:31,713
Lemuria.

42
00:02:31,713 --> 00:02:38,818
And here is the twist: it began as respectable science, and it began with a monkey-like animal.

43
00:02:38,818 --> 00:02:39,566
The lemur.

44
00:02:40,033 --> 00:02:46,073
In the eighteen sixties, the British zoologist Philip Sclater noticed something odd.

45
00:02:46,073 --> 00:02:51,466
Lemur fossils turned up in Madagascar and in India, but nowhere in between.

46
00:02:51,933 --> 00:02:57,827
His explanation: a lost land that once joined them, now under the Indian Ocean.

47
00:02:57,827 --> 00:02:59,394
He called it Lemuria.

48
00:02:59,394 --> 00:03:04,766
It was a fair guess for a time before anyone knew continents could move.

49
00:03:05,233 --> 00:03:07,753
Other Victorians liked the idea.

50
00:03:07,753 --> 00:03:13,423
Darwin's circle used land bridges to explain how animals crossed oceans.

51
00:03:13,423 --> 00:03:17,833
For a few decades, Lemuria sat comfortably in textbooks.

52
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:20,401
Then plate tectonics arrived.

53
00:03:20,401 --> 00:03:21,634
Continents drift.

54
00:03:21,634 --> 00:03:27,577
India broke from Madagascar about eighty-eight million years ago and sailed north.

55
00:03:27,577 --> 00:03:32,433
Lemurs probably crossed from Africa on floating mats of vegetation.

56
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:39,487
Sonar mapping of the Indian Ocean floor found no sunken continent, no drowned bridge.

57
00:03:39,487 --> 00:03:42,044
As science, Lemuria was finished.

58
00:03:42,044 --> 00:03:45,066
As a name, it was just getting started.

59
00:03:45,533 --> 00:03:49,563
Because a name that sounds ancient and lost is irresistible.

60
00:03:49,563 --> 00:03:54,533
And in the eighteen eighties, a new movement was hunting for exactly that.

61
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:04,303
Theosophy, the spiritual society founded by Helena Blavatsky, took the dead scientific idea and gave it a new life.

62
00:04:04,303 --> 00:04:09,400
Lemuria became a lost root race, and a lost homeland of spirit.

63
00:04:09,866 --> 00:04:10,759
Layer three.

64
00:04:10,759 --> 00:04:11,354
The boy.

65
00:04:11,354 --> 00:04:14,404
Yreka, California, eighteen eighty-three.

66
00:04:14,404 --> 00:04:23,033
A seventeen-year-old named Frederick Spencer Oliver was working with a surveying crew near the foot of Mount Shasta.

67
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:31,349
By his own account, he climbed the slopes one evening and met a robed stranger who led him through a door in the mountain.

68
00:04:31,349 --> 00:04:35,466
Inside, he said, lay a secret brotherhood and its sacred temple.

69
00:04:35,933 --> 00:04:44,893
He claimed the spirit of an ancient teacher, who called himself Phylos the Thibetan, then dictated a long story through his hand.

70
00:04:44,893 --> 00:04:48,366
Over three years, Oliver filled hundreds of pages.

71
00:04:48,833 --> 00:04:53,024
The result was a novel-like book, A Dweller on Two Planets.

72
00:04:53,024 --> 00:04:59,132
It tells of a past life in Atlantis, and of survivors hiding under a western mountain.

73
00:04:59,132 --> 00:05:02,399
It was not finished until eighteen eighty-six.

74
00:05:02,866 --> 00:05:05,793
Oliver died young, in eighteen ninety-nine.

75
00:05:05,793 --> 00:05:10,965
It was his mother, Mary, who finally published the book in nineteen oh five.

76
00:05:10,965 --> 00:05:12,666
He never saw it in print.

77
00:05:13,133 --> 00:05:14,803
Here is what matters.

78
00:05:14,803 --> 00:05:18,860
In that book, Lemuria is mentioned only in passing.

79
00:05:18,860 --> 00:05:22,679
The big idea, a Lemurian city, is not there yet.

80
00:05:22,679 --> 00:05:26,099
But Shasta as a place of hidden masters is.

81
00:05:26,566 --> 00:05:27,816
Is that evidence?

82
00:05:27,816 --> 00:05:29,726
It is evidence of a story.

83
00:05:29,726 --> 00:05:32,666
A real book, a real author, a real date.

84
00:05:32,666 --> 00:05:38,766
It shows exactly where the Shasta secret-brotherhood legend first appears in print.

85
00:05:39,233 --> 00:05:42,094
What it is not is evidence of a city.

86
00:05:42,094 --> 00:05:47,276
Oliver described a dream-like vision, written by automatic writing.

87
00:05:47,276 --> 00:05:50,215
No survey, no artifact, no photograph.

88
00:05:50,215 --> 00:05:51,066
Just pages.

89
00:05:51,533 --> 00:05:52,353
Layer four.

90
00:05:52,353 --> 00:05:53,098
The merge.

91
00:05:53,098 --> 00:05:59,432
By the nineteen twenties, the Shasta story and the Lemuria story were still separate.

92
00:05:59,432 --> 00:06:01,966
One writer was about to join them.

93
00:06:02,433 --> 00:06:07,386
Harvey Spencer Lewis led a mystical order in San Jose, California.

94
00:06:07,386 --> 00:06:15,566
In nineteen thirty-one, writing under the pen name Wishar Spenle Cerve, he published a small book on Lemuria.

95
00:06:16,033 --> 00:06:21,683
It described a lost Pacific continent, and survivors who fled to Mount Shasta.

96
00:06:21,683 --> 00:06:27,187
They built a city there, he wrote, with jewelled walls and golden-lit halls.

97
00:06:27,187 --> 00:06:29,433
Later followers named it Telos.

98
00:06:29,899 --> 00:06:38,740
Lewis said the survivors were tall, calm and robed in white, and sometimes came out at dusk to buy supplies in nearby towns.

99
00:06:38,740 --> 00:06:42,233
This is where the modern legend gets its picture.

100
00:06:42,699 --> 00:06:44,765
Notice the order of events.

101
00:06:44,765 --> 00:06:48,513
Science invented Lemuria in the eighteen sixties.

102
00:06:48,513 --> 00:06:53,867
A teenager's book put hidden masters on Shasta in eighteen eighty-six.

103
00:06:53,867 --> 00:06:57,233
A mystic joined them in nineteen thirty-one.

104
00:06:57,699 --> 00:06:59,280
Soon the story spread.

105
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:08,333
Shasta locals began telling visitors about lights on the mountain, strange hikers in white, and Lemurian bells heard at night.

106
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:10,992
Some of those lights are real.

107
00:07:10,992 --> 00:07:17,350
Lenticular clouds, headlights on distant roads, and campfires all look odd at altitude.

108
00:07:17,350 --> 00:07:20,566
But each is explained by something ordinary.

109
00:07:21,033 --> 00:07:26,500
Over the following decades, other authors repeated Lewis, and repeated each other.

110
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:27,833
It is a closed loop.

111
00:07:27,833 --> 00:07:32,699
Each retelling cites an earlier retelling, and the trail ends at one pen.

112
00:07:33,166 --> 00:07:33,910
Layer five.

113
00:07:33,910 --> 00:07:34,654
The search.

114
00:07:34,654 --> 00:07:38,981
If a city lived inside Mount Shasta, would anyone have found it?

115
00:07:38,981 --> 00:07:45,066
Plenty of people have looked, and the mountain is one of the best-studied in the Cascades.

116
00:07:45,533 --> 00:07:52,613
The United States Geological Survey has mapped Shasta's rocks, vents and lava flows in detail.

117
00:07:52,613 --> 00:07:57,735
They describe a stratovolcano built from layers of andesite and ash.

118
00:07:57,735 --> 00:07:59,166
Not a hollow shell.

119
00:07:59,633 --> 00:08:01,731
There are real caves nearby.

120
00:08:01,731 --> 00:08:05,029
Lava tubes carved by flows, some quite long.

121
00:08:05,029 --> 00:08:06,828
Cavers have mapped them.

122
00:08:06,828 --> 00:08:12,300
They are cold, rough, and empty, with no walls, no doors and no carvings.

123
00:08:12,766 --> 00:08:19,022
Earthquake sensors, gravity surveys and heat-flow studies would reveal large hidden chambers.

124
00:08:19,022 --> 00:08:22,453
A city big enough to house thousands would show up.

125
00:08:22,453 --> 00:08:23,933
None of them show one.

126
00:08:24,399 --> 00:08:27,135
What they do show is a living volcano.

127
00:08:27,135 --> 00:08:31,527
The government lists Shasta as a high-threat Cascade volcano.

128
00:08:31,527 --> 00:08:36,566
A hidden city on an active vent would be an unusual choice of address.

129
00:08:37,033 --> 00:08:43,078
Forest rangers, climbers and loggers have worked these slopes for over a hundred and fifty years.

130
00:08:43,078 --> 00:08:49,000
If a robed civilisation were commuting through the woods, someone would have photographed them.

131
00:08:49,466 --> 00:08:55,752
Believers answer that the city exists in another dimension, hidden from ordinary sight.

132
00:08:55,752 --> 00:08:58,137
That claim is impossible to test.

133
00:08:58,137 --> 00:09:01,966
And a claim that cannot be tested cannot be evidence.

134
00:09:02,433 --> 00:09:05,096
That is the fair line for a detective.

135
00:09:05,096 --> 00:09:07,758
We cannot disprove a hidden dimension.

136
00:09:07,758 --> 00:09:12,033
We can only say no measurement has ever found a trace of one.

137
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:13,240
Layer six.

138
00:09:13,240 --> 00:09:14,203
The pilgrims.

139
00:09:14,203 --> 00:09:18,351
Whatever the truth, the legend became real in one sense.

140
00:09:18,351 --> 00:09:26,500
In the nineteen eighties, thousands came to Shasta for the Harmonic Convergence, a world-wide spiritual event.

141
00:09:26,966 --> 00:09:33,455
The nearby town of Mount Shasta now has crystal shops, retreat centres and guided hikes.

142
00:09:33,455 --> 00:09:39,133
For many people, the mountain is a sacred place regardless of what is inside.

143
00:09:39,600 --> 00:09:43,592
Some visitors say they feel calm, energised or visited.

144
00:09:43,592 --> 00:09:48,747
Those feelings are real, and altitude, silence and beauty are powerful.

145
00:09:48,747 --> 00:09:51,433
Science does not claim they are fake.

146
00:09:51,899 --> 00:10:00,209
Since the nineteen nineties, a few speakers have claimed to be in contact with Telos and its leader, called Adama.

147
00:10:00,209 --> 00:10:04,000
Their messages repeat Lewis's images almost exactly.

148
00:10:04,466 --> 00:10:08,839
Stories of white-robed strangers are the oldest of the lot.

149
00:10:08,839 --> 00:10:13,731
But a robe, a beard and a walking stick describe a hundred hikers.

150
00:10:13,731 --> 00:10:17,066
Not one has ever been identified as Lemurian.

151
00:10:17,533 --> 00:10:24,319
Even the claim of Lemurians buying groceries has no named store, no receipt, and no photograph.

152
00:10:24,319 --> 00:10:28,533
It spreads as a story told about someone, never by someone.

153
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,857
There is nothing wrong with loving a story.

154
00:10:31,857 --> 00:10:35,844
The problem comes only when a story is presented as history.

155
00:10:35,844 --> 00:10:39,233
And that is where a detective has to hold the line.

156
00:10:39,700 --> 00:10:43,227
So let us put the last layer of the map in place.

157
00:10:43,227 --> 00:10:48,266
The one that decides whether Lemuria, as a place, ever existed at all.

158
00:10:48,733 --> 00:10:49,592
Layer seven.

159
00:10:49,592 --> 00:10:50,308
The ocean.

160
00:10:50,308 --> 00:10:57,897
Could a continent big enough for a civilisation have sunk in the Pacific within the span of human history?

161
00:10:57,897 --> 00:11:00,833
Geologists say no, for one simple reason.

162
00:11:01,299 --> 00:11:04,821
Continents are made of light granite and sit high.

163
00:11:04,821 --> 00:11:07,709
Ocean floor is dense basalt and sits low.

164
00:11:07,709 --> 00:11:10,808
A continent cannot simply sink into the sea.

165
00:11:10,808 --> 00:11:13,766
It would have to turn into something else.

166
00:11:14,233 --> 00:11:17,475
Sunken land does exist, but on small scales.

167
00:11:17,475 --> 00:11:24,034
Volcanic islands subside, and ice-age coastlines flooded about twelve thousand years ago.

168
00:11:24,034 --> 00:11:29,266
Those events were real, but they were slow, local and recorded in rock.

169
00:11:29,733 --> 00:11:32,319
No drowned continent in the Pacific.

170
00:11:32,319 --> 00:11:37,779
Seafloor dating shows the ocean crust is far younger than any human culture.

171
00:11:37,779 --> 00:11:41,299
Nothing that large vanished while people watched.

172
00:11:41,766 --> 00:11:44,799
So the geological Lemuria is debunked.

173
00:11:44,799 --> 00:11:47,672
The city beneath Shasta is untested.

174
00:11:47,672 --> 00:11:51,184
And the white-robed visitors are unverified.

175
00:11:51,184 --> 00:11:55,733
But one thing is solid: the story has a traceable author.

176
00:11:56,200 --> 00:11:59,162
That is the deepest finding of this case.

177
00:11:59,162 --> 00:12:02,269
Not a lost civilisation, but a paper trail.

178
00:12:02,269 --> 00:12:05,376
A zoologist, a teenage writer and a mystic.

179
00:12:05,376 --> 00:12:08,266
Three people, three books, one mountain.

180
00:12:08,733 --> 00:12:10,511
Time to read the scorecard.

181
00:12:10,511 --> 00:12:14,266
We will score each layer of the map, one claim at a time.

182
00:12:14,733 --> 00:12:15,332
Debrief.

183
00:12:15,332 --> 00:12:19,299
Time to score the legend of Mount Shasta and Lemuria.

184
00:12:19,766 --> 00:12:22,962
A real, towering, active volcano: evidence.

185
00:12:22,962 --> 00:12:29,501
A sacred mountain to its first peoples, and a myth that remembers an eruption: evidence.

186
00:12:29,501 --> 00:12:34,405
Sclater's Lemuria as a scientific guess: true, and since replaced.

187
00:12:34,405 --> 00:12:39,533
Oliver's book as the first Shasta secret-brotherhood story: evidence.

188
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,773
A sunken Pacific continent: debunked.

189
00:12:42,773 --> 00:12:46,821
A Lemurian city inside Shasta: no trace in any survey.

190
00:12:46,821 --> 00:12:53,943
White-robed survivors, jewelled halls and Telos: unverified, and traced to nineteen thirty-one.

191
00:12:53,943 --> 00:12:57,766
The truth about Shasta is more human than the myth.

192
00:12:58,233 --> 00:12:58,839
Verdict.

193
00:12:58,839 --> 00:13:04,822
Mounted on a real volcano, built on a real guess, and told through a real book.

194
00:13:04,822 --> 00:13:08,609
The lost city of Lemuria is a legend, not a place.

195
00:13:08,609 --> 00:13:12,700
But the way it was built is a fascinating human story.

196
00:13:13,166 --> 00:13:16,058
Maybe that is the real power of Shasta.

197
00:13:16,058 --> 00:13:22,140
It is a mountain so big that people have always needed to put something inside it.

198
00:13:22,140 --> 00:13:23,326
Stories, mostly.

199
00:13:23,326 --> 00:13:25,700
And a few pencil lines on a map.

200
00:13:26,166 --> 00:13:32,953
Next case: a stone city in Africa, built with no mortar, and a long argument about who built it.

201
00:13:32,953 --> 00:13:37,266
Why did colonial explorers refuse to believe Africans did it?

202
00:13:37,733 --> 00:13:38,940
What do you think?

203
00:13:38,940 --> 00:13:42,898
Is Lemuria a legend, or a clue to something we are missing?

204
00:13:42,898 --> 00:13:48,533
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
